Solid-state phase transformations toward a metal–organic framework of 7-connected $\mathbf{Zn_{4}O}$ secondary building units
Jaehui Kim, Junsu Ha, Jae Hwa Lee $(\boxtimes)$ , and Hoi Ri Moon $(\boxtimes)$
Department of Chemistry, Ulsan National Institute of Science and Technology (UNIST), Ulsan 44919, Republic of Korea
$\circledcirc$ Tsinghua University Press and Springer-Verlag GmbH Germany, part of Springer Nature 2020   
Received: 4 March 2020 / Revised: 22 April 2020 / Accepted: 11 May 2020
## ABSTRACT
In the development of metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), secondary building units (SBUs) have been utilized as molecular modules for the construction of nanoporous materials with robust structures. Under solvothermal synthetic conditions, dynamic changes in the metal coordination environments and ligand coordination modes of SBUs determine the resultant product structures. Alternatively, MOF phases with new topologies can also be achieved by post-synthetic treatment of as-synthesized MOFs via the introduction of acidic or basic moieties that cause the simultaneous cleavage/reformation of coordination bonds in the solid state. In this sense, we studied the solid-state transformation of two ndc-based Zn-MOFs (ndc $^{=1,4}$ -naphthalene dicarboxylate) with different SBUs but the same pcu topology to another MOF with sev topology. One of the chosen MOFs with pcu nets is $[Z\mathsf{n}_{2}(\mathsf{n d c})_{2}(\mathsf{b p}\mathsf{y})]_{n}$ $\mathsf{\Delta}[\mathsf{b}\mathsf{p}\mathsf{y}=$ $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine), ( $\mathtt{\backslash6C_{\mathrm{{bpy}}}}$ -MOF) consisting of a 6-connected pillared-paddlewheel SBU, and the other is IRMOF-7 composed of 6-connected $Z_{\cap_{4}\mathrm{O}(\mathsf{C O O})_{6}}$ SBUs and ndc. Upon post-structural modification, these pcu MOFs were converted into the same MOF with sev topology constructed from the uncommon 7-connected $Z n_{4}O(C O O)_{7}$ SBU (7C-MOF). The appropriate post-synthetic conditions for the transformation of each SBUs were systematically examined. In addition, the effect of the pillar molecules in the pillared-paddlewheel MOFs on the topology conversion was studied in terms of the linker basicity, which determines the inertness during the solid-state phase transformation. This post-synthetic modification approach is expected to expand the available methods for designing and synthesizing MOFs with controlled topologies.
## KEYWORDS
metal–organic framework, secondary building units, solid-state transformation, linker basicity, ligand addition reaction
## Introduction
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are a class of nanoporous materials, in which single metal ions or clusters are coordinated by multitopic organic linkers [1–3]. MOFs have been popularized owing to the potential structural diversity provided by the vast geometric and compositional variations in inorganic units as well as organic molecules. In MOF chemistry, polynuclear metal clusters and the associated ligand binding groups are commonly referred to as secondary building units (SBUs), which allow highly complex structures to be described in simple terms of nets. The SBUs are generally formed in situ during a solvothermal reaction, contributing to the formation of highly crystalline products via slow and reversible assembly with organic ligands. Advantageously, SBUs can also form more robust MOF structures than mononuclear metal nodes, as the entanglement of metal ions via chelating and bridging by charged linkers leads to the formation of fixed coordination geometries with increased bond strengths and structural directionality [4, 5]. However, because of the structural uncertainty originating from the wide variety of available SBU geometries and their diverse assembly modes, the controllable synthesis of desired MOFs remains challenging and much trial and error is required [2, 3, 6].
In the hydro- or solvothermal reactions that are commonly adapted for MOF syntheses, subtle differences in the reaction parameters such as $\mathrm{\tt{pH}}$ , solvent, molar ratio, and temperature can dramatically alter the SBUs and the topologies of the resultant MOFs, which limit the targeted synthesis of specific MOF phases. To overcome this issue, post-structural transformations in the solid state can be conducted to induce different phases in the obtained MOFs [7–10]. Such studies involve a variety of aspects including the coordination rearrangement of the molecular components, which should be accompanied by the cleavage and regeneration of coordination bonds to generate a new topology. For example, a pillared-paddlewheel MOF with the $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(\mathrm{dabco})]_{n}$ structure (bdc $\mathbf{\Phi}=\mathbf{\Phi}_{1,4}$ -benzene dicarboxylate, dabco $^{=1,4}$ -diazabicycle[2,2,2]octane) undergoes a solid-state transformation from a pillared kagomé-lattice form to a pillared square-lattice form [11]. In this transformation triggered by methanol addition, the OH group of methanol induces the dissociation of the bdc ligand from the SBU while the dabco pillars remain connected. The simultaneous deformation of the metastable kagomé layers gives thermodynamically stable square layers following the reformation of the coordination bonds. Thus, studies on the phase transformations between structurally dissimilar MOFs necessitate an understanding of the intra-cluster behaviors. Another notable example is the phase transformation from MOF-2 to MOF-5 $\left([\mathrm{Zn(bdc)(H_{2}O)}]_{n}\right.$ and $\left[\mathrm{Zn_{4}(\upmu_{4}{-}O)(b d c)_{3}}\right]_{n},$ respectively), reported by Nakamura et al. [12, 13]. Upon heating at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in a $\mathrm{N,N^{\prime}}$ -dimethylformamide (DMF) solution containing $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}.6H_{2}O_{3}}$ MOF-2 comprising planar four-handed dinuclear clusters converts into MOF-5 comprising octahedral six-handed $\mathrm{{\calZno}}$ clusters [12]. From a recent follow-up study, it was demonstrated that the squarefeatured prenucleation clusters of MOF-2 can change into cube-like structures under the synthetic conditions for MOF-5 $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in DMF) [13]. This phenomenon is observed more frequently as the reaction is prolonged and the solution becomes more basic because of the thermal decomposition of DMF; at elevated temperatures, DMF is hydrolyzed to afford formate which can act as a base. The solution basicity contributes to the generation of basic oxide ions $\mathrm{\Delta}^{\cdot}\mathrm{O}^{2-}$ or $Z\mathrm{n^{+}-O^{-}};$ ) [13–17], which can add to the dinuclear cluster in MOF-2 and induce the intra-cluster conversion.
Inspired by these studies of SBU alteration in post-synthetic phase transformation, we chose two MOFs with pcu topologies as model systems. The first is the pillared-paddlewheel MOF $[Z\mathrm{n}_{2}(\mathrm{nd}\mathsf{c})_{2}(\mathrm{bpy})]_{n}$ $6\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{bpy}^{-}}\mathrm{MOF},$ ndc $^{=1,4}$ -naphthalene dicarboxylate, $\mathsf{b p y}=4{,}4^{\prime}$ -bipyridine), which has linear ditopic ligands connected to four-handed paddlewheel SBUs to produce two-dimensional (2D) square-grid sheets that are linked by N-donor pillars links three-dimensional (3D) pcu nets. The second is IRMOF-7 $\left([\mathrm{Zn_{4}O}(\mathrm{ndc})_{3}]_{n}\right)$ , which has 6-connected $Z_{\mathrm{{n_{4}O}(-C O O)_{6}}}$ SBUs. These MOFs with 6-connectivity were transformed into another MOF $\{[\mathrm{Zn_{4}(\mu_{4}{-}O)(n d c)_{3.5}(D M F)}]$ · $[\mathrm{Zn}_{0.5}(\mathrm{DMF})_{3}]{\cdot}2.5\mathrm{DMF}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\}_{n}$ (hereafter, designated as 7C-MOF) with sev topology constructed from the uncommon 7-connected $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn4O(COO)}}_{7}$ SBU (Fig. 1). These transformation phenomena were carefully studied under various conditions to elucidate the critical factors on conversion. Furthermore, the effects of varying the length and basicity of the pillar molecules in the mother MOFs on the structural transformations were also examined.
## 2 Results and discussion
## 2.1 Solid‐state transformation of pcu to sev topology
According to a previously reported procedure [18], $6\mathrm{C_{bpy^{-}}M O F}.$ $[Z\mathrm{n}_{2}(\mathrm{nd}\mathrm{c})_{2}(\mathrm{bpy})]_{n},$ was prepared by the solvothermal reaction of $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}.6H_{2}O},$ ndc, and bpy in DMF, resulting in colorless large cuboid crystals (Fig. 2(a)). In the crystal structure, the dinuclear 6-connected paddlewheel SBU forms a 3D pcu topology with square windows, in which each metal is coordinated by four oxygen atoms from the carboxylates and one nitrogen from the pillar ligand in a square-pyramidal fashion (Fig. 2(a) and Fig. S1 in the Electronic Supplimentary Material (ESM)). Notably, the naphthalene moiety of the ndc ligand is sterically bulky, thus hindering framework interpenetration. As a non-interpenetrated porous framework causes solvents or other reactants to be accessible to the SBUs, the pillars can be replaced or dissociated by other species owing to the metal–N-donor bonds being relatively weak compared with the metal–carboxylate bonds [19–23].
To determine the likelihood of structural transformation, the as-synthesized $6C_{\mathrm{bpy}}$ -MOF crystals $\mathrm{\Omega}^{\left\langle30\ m g\right\rangle}$ ) were placed in a $5~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF solution of $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}.6H_{2}O}$ ( $\sim5$ equivalent to each paddle-wheel SBU) and heated at $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $36\mathrm{~h~}$ . Here, the $Z\mathrm{n}(\mathrm{II})$ precursors was added to drive the formation of basic oxide ions, which in the previous MOF-2 system was observed to cause framework decomposition into small assemblies and the reconstruction of a new framework from the existing building blocks around the SBU in an intermediate state [13, 24]. After the heat treatment, the single crystal of $6\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{bpy}^{-}}\mathrm{MOF}$ turned into fractured polycrystalline cuboid (Fig. 2(b)), but the contained crystals were still large enough to collect single-crystal X-ray diffraction (SCXRD) data. A comparision of the powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns of $6\mathrm{C_{bpy^{-}}M O F}$ and the converted solid indicates the complete disappearance of the peaks of $6C_{\mathrm{bpy}}$ -MOF and the appearance of new peaks (Fig. 2(d)). Based on the SCXRD data, in the converted solid, the paddlewheel SBU in $6\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{bpy}}–\mathrm{MOF}$ was completely transformed into $\mathrm{Zno}$ clusters, which were coordinated by seven carboxylates from seven different ndc ligands and one terminal DMF solvent molecule (Fig. 2(b) and Fig. S1 in the ESM). The resultant compound is $\{[\mathrm{Zn_{4}(\mu_{4}-O)(n d c)_{3.5}(D M F)}]{\cdot}[\mathrm{Zn_{0.5}(D M F)_{3}}]$ · $2.5\mathrm{DMF}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\}_{n}$ (7C-MOF), which crystallizes in the orthogonal space group Cmca and has a sev net topology (Table S1 in the ESM). Notably, the observation of a 7-connected SBU is very rare in the MOF field, with only few examples reported in the literature [25–29].
Interestingly, when no zinc precusors or very small amounts ( $\sim$ 0.5 equivalents to each SBU) were introduced, the postsynthetic heat treatment did not lead to a phase transformation and the $6C_{\mathrm{bpy}}$ -MOF crystals remained almost intact, as revealed by an optical microscope and PXRD patterns (Figs. S2 and S3 in the ESM). These results confirm that the presence of sufficient basic oxide ions are essential to facilitate the deformation of the SBU structure and change the overall topology in $6C_{\mathrm{bpy}}$ -MOF. Further, when the reaction was conducted with added H2ndc ligands instead of $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ , no structural transformation occured, although solvent-assisted ligand exchange or an addition reaction with the SBUs could be expected [30]. Generally, upon acidification, the dislocation of metals and carboxylates in SBUs via the cleavage of $Z\mathrm{n-O}$ coordination bonds is entropically favored. However, in this case, the structure of $6C_{\mathrm{bpy}}$ -MOF might be thermodynamically stable enough to withstand the acidity of the H2ndc ligands (Fig. S4 in the ESM).
To further clarify the role of the synthetic precursors in the solid-state transformation, the transformation of another MOF with pcu topology, IRMOF-7 into 7C-MOF was investigated. IRMOF-7, which is composed of octahedrally 6-connected $\mathrm{Zn_{4}O(C O O)_{6}}$ SBUs with ndc ligands, and 7C-MOF have the same $Z_{\mathrm{{n4}}}\mathrm{{O}}.$ -based SBUs, except the latter has one more carboxylate connected to the $\mathrm{\Delta}Z\mathrm{n}_{4}\mathrm{O}$ cluster to form a $\mathrm{Zn_{4}O(C O O)_{7}}$ . The IRMOF-7 structure consists of $\mathrm{Zn4O}$ tetrahedra at the corners of a cube linked by six carboxylate bridges (octahedral SBUs), resulting in primitive cubic structure that gives a pcu net. Soaking the as-synthesized transparent IRMOF-7 crystals $\left(46~\mathrm{mg}\right)$ in a $5~\mathrm{mL}$ DMF solution of H2ndc ( $\cdot\sim2$ equivalent to each SBU) and heating at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ resulted in a successful transformation into an opaque crystal in a single-crystal-tosingle-crystal manner (Fig. 2(c)). The structure of the converted solid was consistent with that of 7C-MOF based on the PXRD patterns (Fig. 2(e)). Unlike the 6-connected pillared-paddlewheel SBUs, the $\mathrm{{Zn4O}}$ -based SBUs of IRMOF-7 were already identical to those of 7C-MOF; therefore, the addition of a basic species was not required to form the $\mathrm{{znO}}$ cluster of 7C-MOF. Instead, to realize the transformation from the 6-connected to the
7-connected $\mathrm{Zn_{4}O}$ -based SBU, an additional ndc ligand was required to compensate for the lack of $Z\mathrm{n}\cdot$ –carboxylate connectivity. Thus, an acidic environment was considered to influence the transformation. This result can be also supported by previous simulation results performed by Zhou et al. based on the model of basic zinc acetate $\mathrm{\small{[Zn_{4}O(C H_{3}C O O)_{6}]}}$ [26], which points out that the formation of a 7-connected unit with an additional acetate ligand is thermodynamically preferred.
## 2.2  Linker effect on topology conversion
Further insight into the transformation of $6\mathrm{C_{L}}$ -MOF $\mathrm{{\dot{L}=}}$ pillar molecules) was gained by performing the same reaction using an isoreticular (same topological) series of pillared-paddlewheel MOFs with different pillar ligands, namely, dabco and $\mathbf{N},\mathbf{N}^{\prime}.$ - di-4-pyridylnaphthalenetetracarboxydiimide (dpndi) (Figs. 3(a) and 3(b)). Compared with the bpy ligand, these two ligands have dramatically different chemical properties. For instance, dabco is shorter and more basic than bpy, whereas dpndi is much longer and relatively acidic. The calculated $\mathrm{{pK}_{a}}$ values of the conjugated (monoprotonated) acid are 9.76, 5.25, and 3.92 for dabco, bpy, and dpndi, respectively [31]. As it is known that linker basicity can determine the relative metal–ligand bond strength [21], we postulated that the more basic pillars would render the corresponding pillared-paddlewheel structures fairly “inert” to the solid-state phase transformation.
Accordingly, unlike the case of $6C_{\mathrm{bpy}}$ -MOF, the solvothermal treatment of the crystals of $[Z\mathrm{n}_{2}(\mathrm{nd}{\mathrm{c}})_{2}(\mathrm{dab}\mathrm{c}{\mathrm{o}})]_{n}$ ( $6C_{\mathrm{dabco}}$ -MOF) in a DMF solution of $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}.6H_{2}O}$ did not engender the phase transformation to 7C-MOF, even under harsher conditions (higher $Z n(N O_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ concentration $\mathit{\Pi}_{\sim\ 20}$ equivalent) or higher temperature $\left(100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\right)_{\cdot}$ ). The single crystals retained their integrity, with the exception of slight dissolution near the surface, and the PXRD patterns showed no changes (Figs. 3(c)
and 3(e), and Fig. S5 in the ESM). On the other hand, compared with the other pillared-paddlewheel MOFs, $[Z\mathrm{n}_{2}(\mathrm{n}\mathrm{d}\mathrm{c})_{2}(\mathrm{d}\mathrm{p}\mathrm{n}\mathrm{d}\mathrm{i})]_{n}$ $(6\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{dpndi}}–\mathrm{MOF})$ has much longer pillars (15.450, 2.569, and $7.04\dot{3}\mathrm{~\AA~}$ for dpndi, dabco, and bpy, respectively, as estimated between the centers of terminal $_\mathrm{~N~}$ atoms from the relevant crystallographic information files) and their conjugate acid has the relatively low $\mathrm{{pK}_{a}}$ (i.e., weaker base). Accordingly, even under lower concentration of $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}.6H_{2}O}$ (0.5 equilvalent), 6Cdpndi-MOF was transformed into 7C-MOF (Figs. 3(d) and 3(f), and Fig. S6 in the ESM). However, during the heat treatment, the $6\mathrm{C_{dpndi}}$ -MOF crystals mostly dissolved over $36\mathrm{h},$ remaining only a very small amounts of 7C-MOF crystals. This difference in the reaction outcomes of $6{\mathrm{C}}_{\mathrm{dabco}^{-}}{\mathrm{MOF}}$ and $6\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{dpndi}}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{MOF}$ reveals that the transformation of the pillaredpaddlewheel SBUs needs to proceed through the removal of the pillars so that additional building units can be integrated for conversion to the $\mathrm{Zn_{4}O}$ -based units. Thus, the pillar basicity determines the SBU inertness and will also govern whether the original crystals exhibit the structural maintenance, building unit dissolution, or the solid-state transformation.
As the pillar molecule effect on the topology conversion from pcu to sev could be explored by dabco and dpndi with intact and forward results, it would be also beneficial to examine the effect of carboxylates on the same reaction. Thus, we prepared two additional pillared-paddlewheel MOFs, $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(\mathrm{bpy})]_{n}$ and $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(2,6\mathrm{-ndc})_{2}(\mathrm{bpy})]_{n}$ $[2,6\mathrm{-ndc}~=~2,6$ -naphthalene dicarboxylate) [32, 33], and observed their phase transition behaviors on the same solvothermal treatment. It should be noted that those MOFs have the same pcu topology as $6C_{\mathrm{bpy}}$ -MOF but are doubly interpenetrated because the bdc and 2,6-ndc linkers have no sterically bulky substituents to hinder the framework interpenetration [34, 35]. In the case of the bdc-based MOF, under the solvothermal reaction with $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}.6H_{2}O},$ the structure converted to MOF-5 of pcu topology, instead of the sev net with the 7-connected SBUs (Fig. S7 in the ESM). The transformation was triggered in the same basic media with $Z\mathrm{n}$ precursors like $6\mathrm{C}_{\mathrm{bpy}^{-}}\mathrm{MOF}$ but resulted in the topologically different compound because the bdc and ndc linkers have different potential coordination modes reconstructing new frameworks. On the other hand, $[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}(2,6\mathrm{-ndc})_{2}(\mathrm{bpy})]_{n}$ did not undergo any phase transition upon heating in a DMF solution, whether with or without additives (Fig. S8 in the ESM). This is attributed to the improved stability of the SBUs from higher $\mathrm{pK_{a}}$ level of 2,6-ndc and/or the 3-fold interpenetrated framework causing more steric hinderances to ligand displacement [36]. These results reveal that the carboxylate ligand is also the important parameter to influence the phase transformation from pcu to sev topology.
## 2.3  Crystal structure and characterization of 7C‐MOF
7C-MOF is composed of the rare 7-connected SBU, and only few MOFs with it have been reported so far [25–29]. Liu et al. recently reported the same topological structure with the present 7C-MOF [25], in which the same $Z\mathrm{n}^{2+}$ and ndc2− ligands were used as building blocks but the bridging oxygen in the SBU was differently assigned in the crystal structure ( $\upmu_{4}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{OH}$ instead of $\upmu_{4}-\mathrm{O})$ ). In the crystal structure of 7C-MOF, the $\mathrm{Zn_{4}O}$ clusters are bound by seven carboxylate anions from seven different ndc ligands and one terminal DMF solvent molecule (Fig. 2(b) and Fig. S1 in the ESM). In the SBU, two $Z\mathrm{n}$ ions were located in four-coordinated tetragonal geometries, while the other two $Z\mathrm{n}$ ions were in a five-coordinated square pyramidal geometry and a six-coordinated distorted octahedral geometry because of the coordinated DMF molecule. The charge imbalance of the 7-connected SBU caused a net $^{-1}$ charge per formular unit, which was compensated by half a $[\mathrm{Zn(DMF)}_{6}]^{2+}$ cation residing in the pores (Fig. S9 in the ESM), as evidenced by the element analysis (EA) and SCXRD data. This centrosymmetric cation possessed two crystallographically independent DMF molecules in the asymmetric unit (Fig. S9 in the ESM).
Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) revealed that heating 7C-MOF removed the coordinated DMF molecules as well as the guest solvents, with an experimentally determined weight loss of $32.3\%$ between room temperature and $\sim270^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ , which is in good agreement with the calculated value of $32.5\%$ (6.5 DMF and $2\ \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules per formula unit) (Fig. S10 in the ESM). Because thermal treatment at a high temperature $(270^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ caused irreversible structural deformation with a low degree of crystallinity (Fig. S11 in the ESM), for pore activation the non-coordinating solvent molecules were removed under vacuum at room temperature, which was confirmed by NMR (Fig. S12 in the ESM). As shown in Fig. S13(a) in the ESM, based on the $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ sorption isotherm, the sample showed no porosity. However, up to $1.43\ \mathrm{mmol/g}$ of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ gas could be adsorbed at $196\mathrm{~K~}$ in a stepwise manner without affecting the phase, as confirmed by the PXRD patterns before and after the gas sorption experiment (Fig. S13(b) in the ESM). This adsorption behavior is presumably due to an electrostatic field generated by the charged framework and counterion of $[Z\mathrm{n(DMF)}_{6}]^{2+}$ in the pore [37, 38].
## 3 Conclusion
We have demonstrated the solid-state transformation of two different ndc-based $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOFs with pcu topology based on 6-connected SBUs into the sev topology that consists of unique 7-connected $\mathrm{Zn_{4}O}$ SBUs. For the pillared-paddlewheel MOFs, the transformation is favored in basic media in the presence of the $Z\mathrm{n}$ precursor that confirms the significance of basic oxide ions, which act as both a base and a building source during SBU alteration. IRMOF-7, composed of the same ndc ligands and the classic octahedrally 6-connected $\mathrm{Zn_{4}O(C O O)_{6}}$ SBU, also underwent transformation into the same MOF of 7-connected $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{Zn_{4}O(C O O)_{7}}}$ SBUs via a ligand addition reaction that induces structural deformation toward a thermodynamically more stable framework. In addition, exploration of the same transformative route while varying the length and basicity of the ligands revealed that more basic pillars hindered the solid-state transformation. These insights into the parameters that drive the successful conversion of each SBU enrich the understanding of the SBU-based post-synthetic modification approach and can potentially be expanded to other MOFs to realize the targeted design and synthesis of various topologies.
## 4 Experimental
## 4.1  Materials and instruments
All chemicals and solvents were of reagent grade and were used as received without further purification. The dpndi was prepared according to the method previously reported [39]. Optical microscopic images were recorded on a Nikon SMZ745T microscope equipped with TrueChrome Metrics camera. NMR spectra were recorded on Agilent 400-MR DD2 spectrometer. PXRD patterns were recorded on a Bruker D2 phaser diffractometer at $30\mathrm{kV}$ and $10\mathrm{mA}$ for $\operatorname{Cu}\operatorname{K}\alpha$ $\langle\lambda=1.54\dot{0}50\ \mathring\mathrm{A};$ ), with a step size of $0.02^{\circ}$ in 2θ. $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sorption isotherms of samples were obtained using a BELSORP-max at 77 and $196\mathrm{K},$ respectively. Prior to the adsorption measurements, the samples were evacuated $\cdot p<10^{-5}$ mbar) at room temperature (RT) for $36\mathrm{~h~}$ . TGA were performed under $\mathrm{N}_{2}(\mathbf{g})$ atmosphere at a scan rate of $5~{^\circ}\mathrm{C}/{\mathrm{min}}$ using Q50 from TA instruments. Elemental analyses were done by UNIST Central Research Facilities center (UCRF) in Ulsan National Institute of Science and Technology (UNIST).
## 4.2  Synthesis of ${\bf60976}{\bf60F}$
According to a previously reported procedure [18] with slight modification, $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}.6H_{2}O}$ $(74~\mathrm{~mg},~0.25~\mathrm{~mmol})$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{nd}\mathrm{c}$ $(54\mathrm{mg},0.25\mathrm{mmol})$ and bpy $(19\mathrm{mg},0.125\mathrm{mmol})$ were dissolved in DMF $\mathrm{\Delta}5~\mathrm{mL}$ ). The solution was placed in a $35~\mathrm{mL}$ glass jar and capped tightly. The glass jar was heated at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ oven for $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The mother liquor was decanted and resultant crystals were washed with fresh DMF several times.
## 4.3  Transformation of ${\bf607576}{\bf607}$ to 7C‐MOF
$30\mathrm{mg}\left(0.042\mathrm{mmol}\right)$ of the corresponding $6C_{\mathrm{bpy}}$ -MOF was placed in a $35\mathrm{mL}$ glass jar followed by addition of DMF solution (5 mL) of $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(62~\mathrm{~mg},~0.21~\mathrm{~mmol})$ . The mixture was placed at $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ oven and heated for $36\mathrm{h}$ . Yield: $20~\mathrm{mg}$ $(35\%)$ . Anal. calculated for $\mathrm{Zn_{4.5}C_{61.5}H_{70.5}N_{6.5}O_{23.5}}\mathrm{.}$ C, 47.01; H, 4.52; N, 5.80.; found: C, 46.74; H, 4.27; N, 5.85.
## 4.4  Synthesis of 6Cdabco‐MOF
According to a previously reported procedure [40] with slight modification, $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}.6H_{2}O}$ (476 mg, 1.6 mmol), H2ndc $(346~\mathrm{mg},1.6~\mathrm{mmol})$ were dissolved in DMF $20~\mathrm{mL}$ ). Add dabco $(90\mathrm{mg},0.8\mathrm{mmol})$ to the solution. Separate supernatant solution with white precipitate by centrifuge. The solution was placed in a $35~\mathrm{mL}$ glass jar and capped tightly. The glass jar was heated at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ . The mother liquor was decanted and resultant crystals were washed with fresh DMF several times.
## 4.5  Transformation of 6Cdabco‐MOF to 7C‐MOF
$30~\mathrm{mg}$ ( $\left(0.045\mathrm{\mmol}\right),$ of the corresponding $6C_{\mathrm{dabco}}$ -MOF was placed in a $35~\mathrm{\mL}$ glass jar followed by addition of DMF solution $\mathrm{\Delta}5~\mathrm{mL}$ ) of $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}.6H_{2}O}$ $268~\mathrm{mg}$ , 0.9 mmol). The mixture was placed at $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ oven and heated for $36\mathrm{h}$ .
## 4.6  Synthesis of 6Cdpndi‐MOF
According to a previously reported procedure [41] with slight modification, $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}.6H_{2}O}$ $\cdot12\mathrm{mg},0.04\mathrm{mmol},$ , H2ndc (9 mg, $0.04\ \mathrm{mmol};$ ) and dpndi $(17~\mathrm{mg},0.04~\mathrm{mmol})$ were dissolved in N,N’-diethylformamide (DEF) $(5~\mathrm{mL})$ ). The solution was placed in a $35~\mathrm{mL}$ glass jar and capped tightly. The glass jar was heated at $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ oven for $^{48\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The mother liquor was decanted and resultant crystals were washed with fresh DEF several times.
## 4.7  Transformation of 6Cdpndi‐MOF to 7C‐MOF
$30~\mathrm{mg}$ $\left(0.031\ \mathrm{mmol}\right)$ of the corresponding $6\mathrm{C_{\mathrm{dpndi}}\mathrm{-}M O F}$ was placed in a $35~\mathrm{mL}$ glass jar followed by addition of DMF solution $(5~\mathrm{mL})$ of $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}.6H_{2}O}$ $(45\mathrm{mg},0.15\mathrm{mmol})$ . The mixture was placed at $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ oven and heated for $36\mathrm{h}$ . Yield: $1.5\mathrm{mg}\left(5\%\right)$ .
## 4.8  Synthesis of IRMOF‐7
According to a previously reported procedure [42] with slight modification, $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}.6H_{2}O}$ $(119\mathrm{~mg,~}0.40\mathrm{~mmol})$ , H2ndc $(87~\mathrm{mg},0.40~\mathrm{mmol})$ were dissolved in DEF $\mathrm{\Delta}5\mathrm{mL},$ . The solution was placed in glass jar and capped tightly. The glass jar was heated at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ oven for $24\mathrm{h}$ . 'The mother liquor was decanted and resultant crystals were washed with fresh DMF several times for use in the next transformation reaction.
